        "title": "Determining the Lengths of the Major and Minor Axes Given the Equation of an Ellipse"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "3a6ef55b-f35c-5e25-8e8b-c766bf7961eb",
        ":question/problem": "For the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\frac{82}{9}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{70}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=\\frac{82}{9}$ and ${b}^{2}=70.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=\\frac{82}{9},$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{\\frac{82}{9}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{82}}{3}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=70,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{70}.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $b=\\sqrt{70},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=\\frac{\\sqrt{82}}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot \\sqrt{70}=2\\sqrt{70},$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{82}}{3}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{82}}{3}.$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
